// Data of the concept graph (concepts.html): concepts (definitions) of mathematical physics, MSC 2020 70 (mechanics of
// particles and systems), 74 (mechanics of deformable solids), 76 (fluid mechanics), 78 (optics, electromagnetic
// theory), 80 (classical thermodynamics), 81 (quantum theory), 82 (statistical mechanics), 83 (relativity and
// gravitational theory), 85 (astronomy and astrophysics) and 86 (geophysics). In English. Built on 3 November 2026.
//
// Same model as ../../analysis/data/concepts.js: `area` is one of the groupings in `groupAreas`, one per main MSC field
// (74 and 76 share "continuum", 85 and 86 share "astrophysics"); `msc5` is the five-character class. Every concept that
// a definition (name, formula, defining prose) uses explicitly is in `uses`.
//
// The variational side of mechanics (Lagrangian as integrand, action, Euler–Lagrange equation, Hamiltonian as Legendre
// transform, Hamilton–Jacobi equation, variational symmetries and conserved quantities) belongs to the calculus of
// variations ("cv:", 49, 49S); symplectic manifolds, Hamiltonian vector fields and moment maps to geometry ("geo:",
// 53D); Hilbert spaces and operators to analysis ("ana:"). A few concepts whose home folder exists but does not have
// them yet stand here with the area "basic" and their own class (cotangent bundle 58A, trace-class and unbounded
// self-adjoint operators 47B, one-parameter unitary groups 47D, Lorentzian manifold 53C); they should move.
// References: set theory "st:", elementary number theory "ent:", group theory "gt:", commutative algebra "ca:",
// combinatorics "co:", representation theory "rt:", topology "top:", analysis "ana:", geometry "geo:", calculus of
// variations "cv:", dynamical systems "ds:" (integrable systems, invariant tori), differential equations "de:",
// probability "pr:" (percolation, self-avoiding walk), linear algebra "la:".
// Prefix for references from other folders: "mp:<id>".
// Sources: Arnold, Mathematical Methods of Classical Mechanics (2nd ed. 1989); Abraham and Marsden, Foundations of
// Mechanics (2nd ed. 1978); Marchioro and Pulvirenti, Mathematical Theory of Incompressible Nonviscous Fluids (1994);
